13. Import Tariff: Partial and General Equilibrium Effects, Optimum Tariff
Paper: International Economics (ECNCC301) | Unit: III | Topic: Partial equilibrium effects of an import tariff, General equilibrium effects of an import tariff, Optimum tariff
Introduction
A tariff is a tax on imports. It is the oldest and most common trade barrier. Its effects can be analysed in partial equilibrium (a single market) and general equilibrium (the whole economy).
Partial equilibrium: small country
For a small country facing a fixed world price P_w, a tariff t raises the domestic price to P_w + t. Effects:
• Consumption falls (consumer surplus lost).
• Domestic production rises (producer surplus gained).
• Imports fall.
• Government earns revenue = t x imports.
The net loss is two triangles: the production (protection) cost from inefficient domestic production, and the consumption cost from distorted consumption. Net welfare loss = production triangle + consumption triangle.
Partial equilibrium: large country
A large country influences world price. A tariff reduces its import demand, so the world price falls and part of the tariff is paid by foreign exporters. The terms-of-trade gain may exceed the two deadweight triangles, so the country can gain. The domestic price rises by less than the tariff.
Concepts
• Nominal tariff versus effective rate of protection: the effective rate measures protection on value added: EPR = (t_i - sum a_ij t_j) / (1 - sum a_ij). Tariff escalation gives high protection on processed goods.
General equilibrium effects
In a small country, a tariff shifts relative prices toward the import-competing good, production moves toward it along the PPF, and the community indifference curve falls to a lower level: welfare declines. Revenue redistributed to consumers shifts consumption. The Stolper-Samuelson theorem implies the scarce factor gains and the abundant factor loses. The Metzler paradox is a possible case where the tariff lowers the domestic price of the protected good (large country, inelastic demand).
Optimum tariff
For a large country, the optimum tariff maximises welfare by balancing the terms-of-trade gain against the volume-of-trade loss. Its formula is t* = 1/e, where e is the foreign elasticity of export supply. The tariff is zero for a small country (e is infinite). Retaliation by partners may leave everyone worse off, a prisoner's dilemma that motivates trade agreements.
Conclusion
A tariff lowers welfare for a small country but may raise it for a large one at its partners' expense, so international agreement is needed to avoid tariff wars.